{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh: read the plain-language rewrite of docs/reference/architecture.md at exact clean pushed head 1f0411e7b8949374d1cca659e759eda45a70dad0, on branch request/architecture-plain-language, and ratify codex's approval if it satisfies you.\n\nTHIS ROW SHOULD HAVE EXISTED TWO HOURS AGO. dcffa4c8 says \"hugh review and ratification before merge — hugh wants to read this one.\" I have been reporting that the head awaits you since codex's verdict landed, and there was nothing on your board saying so. A dependency I only stated in my own records is not a dependency anyone can act on, which is the exact defect I spent tonight repairing in internal/statusview. I am filing it now rather than letting it keep sitting.\n\nWHAT IT IS. planner's dcffa4c8 relayed your ruling that this was the highest-priority item from the external-review triage: the page was ~870 lines reading like a legal instrument, explaining rules by narrating the bug that motivated them, and you wanted a rewrite rather than a plain-English preface. This is that rewrite.\n\nThe page now opens each layer with what it owns, then what it must never do, then which package holds it. Rules are stated as rules with their consequence. Measured: longest sentence 121 words to 49; sentences over 55 words, 8 to 0; over 40 words, 27 to 13. It grows from 946 to 1093 lines, because 23 subheadings break forty-line paragraphs into findable units. It did not get shorter and I am not claiming it did.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY CHECKED, so you can spend your reading on the prose rather than the mechanics. Codex approved independently at f82c8fe1, which I ratified: front matter and all 14 citations byte-identical, layers 1-7 intact, the \"Application host binding\" heading verbatim because two Go files cite it by name, all 18 package-boundary rows, the six compatibility axes, the review rule, and the request-host admission boundary present and exact. Codex ran its own gates including internal/docset with -count=1.\n\nTwo contract errors were found and fixed before that verdict: a draft had turned a restrictive clause (\"the artifacts that approval itself cites, each standing at the approved head\") into a gate (\"must stand at\"), converting an excluded artifact into a violating one; and the DNS-rebinding guard merged into main mid-rewrite, changing this same page, so publishing unrebased would have silently reverted the request-host boundary.\n\nWHAT I THINK IS WORTH YOUR JUDGEMENT, since the mechanical checks are done and yours is the question they cannot answer: is it actually readable now? My honest assessment is that it is substantially better but not uniform. A newcomer gets a real entry path — seven \"what it owns\" lines are skimmable in a minute, and the resident ownership split, the configuration custody contract and the host identity model each stopped being a wall of text. Where it stays hard is layer 5's Workroom section and the fold-version bumps: that material is densely interlocking, and because no rule could be allowed to shift I kept wording close to the original wherever compression risked meaning. Those parts still read as specification rather than explanation. If that is not good enough, say so and I will take another pass at exactly those sections.\n\nTWO DELIBERATE REMOVALS for you to accept or reject: the count of records whose meaning changes at the workroom-fold@8 migration, and the note that redaction was evaluated before being rejected. Both underlying rules survive; only the narration went. Codex judged both safe. A maintainer verifying that migration might want the tally back and I would not argue.\n\nNothing is blocked on this beyond the merge itself, which is mine once you ratify. If you would rather not read it now, say so and I will hold — I would rather it wait than be merged unread when you asked to see it.","body":{"conditions":"Read docs/reference/architecture.md at exact clean pushed head 1f0411e7b8949374d1cca659e759eda45a70dad0 and judge whether it meets the plain-language rewrite you asked for in the external-review triage relayed by dcffa4c8. Satisfaction is your ratification of codex's approval f82c8fe1, or a statement of what still reads badly. The mechanical conditions are already verified by codex and need not be re-checked: front matter and 14 citations byte-identical, layers 1-7, the exact 'Application host binding' heading, 18 package-boundary rows, six compatibility axes, the review rule, the request-host admission boundary, and docset green with -count=1. Accept or reject the two deliberate history removals named in the request text. If sections remain hard to read, name them and I will take another pass rather than merging. Claude merges after ratification.","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}